window.QUIZ_ANSWERS={"Q1-01": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>1. Q1-01 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which observations about distance metrics are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. L1 adds absolute coordinate differences; B. L-infinity takes the largest absolute difference; D. L2 is ordinary straight-line distance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Different metrics encode different geometries and can change which observations are nearest. They agree in some special cases, not universally.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-02": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>2. Q1-02 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which activities belong to model selection and must avoid using the final test outcomes?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Choosing k for k-NN; C. Choosing an SVM kernel; D. Choosing a classification threshold</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Tuning neighbors, kernels, and thresholds uses outcome information to choose a procedure. Final testing follows that selection and should not trigger more tuning.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-03": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>3. Q1-03 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For objective sum(hinge loss)+lambda*norm(w) squared, what happens as lambda becomes larger, all else equal?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The objective places relatively more emphasis on margin regularization; D. The penalty on large coefficient norms increases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Lambda weights the norm penalty in this convention. Larger lambda may accept poorer training fit for a wider-margin solution; it does not directly multiply hinge loss. Here lambda multiplies the norm penalty. Larger lambda favors a smaller coefficient norm and, under the normalized margin interpretation, a wider margin while allowing more hinge loss. It is not the same placement as software C multiplying hinge loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-04": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>4. Q1-04 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression-tree leaf has training responses 10, 14, and 18. Under ordinary squared-error CART, what is its prediction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 14</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The constant minimizing squared error is the mean, 14. Model trees can use regressions in leaves, but ordinary CART need not do so.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q1-05": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>5. Q1-05 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A city needs different daytime and nighttime patrol locations. Which changes are analytically reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Check patrol coverage and budgets for each period; C. Cluster nighttime incidents separately; D. Cluster daytime incidents separately</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Different time regimes can have different spatial distributions. Separate clusterings can align patrol placement with when incidents occur; sample size alone does not make pooling appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-06": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>6. Q1-06 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An engineer wants an alarm for a sustained increase in axle temperature rather than a future price forecast. Which lecture method directly serves this purpose?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. CUSUM</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CUSUM accumulates evidence of a mean shift over ordered observations. The other methods address dimension reduction, grouping, or counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-07": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>7. Q1-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A logistic model has linear predictor eta=0. What probability does it return?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. .5</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The logistic function is 1/(1+exp(-eta)). At zero, the denominator equals two.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Logistic probabilities versus odds:</strong> Convert eta to p=1/(1+exp(-eta)). Odds=p/(1-p); a one-unit predictor change multiplies odds by exp(beta) in an additive logistic model. It does not multiply probability by the same factor. A probability model and a classification threshold are separate choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-08": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>8. Q1-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about single exponential smoothing are correct for 0&lt;alpha&lt;1?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Recent observations receive larger weights than distant ones; C. Older observations remain represented in the previous level; D. A larger alpha follows new observations more quickly</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The recurrence compresses past information with geometrically declining weights. Single smoothing has no explicit trend or seasonal component.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-09": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>9. Q1-09 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hard-margin linear SVM uses f(x)=b+w&#x27;x and labels ±1. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Minimizing norm(w) squared widens the margin; C. Training constraints can be written y_i f(x_i)&gt;=1; D. The full margin is 2/norm(w)</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The ±1 normalization joins both class constraints. The coefficient norm governs margin width; the intercept shifts the separator and need not vanish. The margin boundaries f=±1 follow the chosen scaling of (w,b). The classification boundary is f=0. Geometric distance to that boundary is abs(f(x))/norm(w), so the two-sided margin width is 2/norm(w); a raw score is not a probability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-10": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>10. Q1-10 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classifier labels positive whenever x1 exceeds 4, regardless of x2. Which feature is irrelevant to this particular rule?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. x2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The boundary is vertical when x1 is horizontal. Varying x2 does not cross it; this is a model-specific conclusion, not a universal claim about the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-11": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>11. Q1-11 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With level 80, new observation 100, and alpha=.25, what is the new single-smoothed level?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 85</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The update is .25*100+.75*80=85. A modest alpha limits how strongly one new observation moves the level.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-12": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>12. Q1-12 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classifier makes 3 false negatives costing $100 each and 10 false positives costing $2 each. Correct decisions have zero cost. What is total cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. $320</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiply each type of mistake by its cost and add: 3*100+10*2=$320. Counting mistakes without their different costs can rank models incorrectly.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-13": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>13. Q1-13 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Observations are recorded once per manufactured component, with a repeatable effect across each 120-component batch. What is the seasonal cycle length?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 120 observations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cycle length is measured in observation units. A component-indexed analysis repeats after 120 components, even if batch duration varies in calendar time.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-14": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>14. Q1-14 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A slope estimate is 6 with standard error 2. For a zero-slope null, what is its t-statistic?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The t-statistic is (estimate-null value)/standard error, or 6/2=3. The sign and magnitude describe deviation from the null in standard-error units.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-15": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>15. Q1-15 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For a training point, y_i f(x_i)=0.4. What is its hinge loss max(0,1-y_i f(x_i))?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 0.6</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The prediction has the correct sign but lies inside the margin. Hinge loss therefore remains positive; it is not just a misclassification indicator.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-16": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>16. Q1-16 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An analyst says “we use regression,” then names predictors, then supplies fitted coefficients. Which statements about the word model are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It can mean a method family; C. It can mean a predictor specification; D. It can mean a fitted equation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lectures use model at all three levels. Clarify whether a question concerns the family, specification, or fitted numerical instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q1-17": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>17. Q1-17 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With disease defined as positive, TP=36, FN=4, FP=12, TN=48. What is sensitivity?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. .90</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sensitivity conditions on actual disease: 36/(36+4)=.90. Precision instead conditions on positive predictions and equals 36/48=.75.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q1-18": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>18. Q1-18 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ordinary PCA are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It uses predictors without response labels; B. It produces orthogonal loading directions; C. It ranks components by predictor variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA is unsupervised. Maximizing predictor variance and maximizing usefulness for a response are different objectives.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-19": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>19. Q1-19 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hospital asks which scheduling action minimizes waiting while respecting staff limits. Which type of analytics answers the final question?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Prescriptive</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Prescriptive analysis selects an action under objectives and constraints. Forecasting arrivals can support the decision but does not itself choose a schedule.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q1-20": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>20. Q1-20 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In upper CUSUM, which changes generally make the alarm less sensitive?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Increase the threshold T; B. Increase the allowance C</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A larger allowance reduces accumulated evidence and a larger threshold requires more evidence. Resetting every period discards the cumulative mechanism rather than tuning it correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-21": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>21. Q1-21 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which value of the Box-Cox power corresponds to the logarithmic limit?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. lambda=0</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The limit of (y to the lambda minus one)/lambda as lambda approaches zero is log(y), for positive y.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-22": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>22. Q1-22 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Optimal WSS drops as k rises all the way to one cluster per distinct point. Why does selecting the smallest WSS alone fail?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. It mechanically favors increasingly fine partitions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> At the extreme, each distinct observation can be its own center with zero WSS. Use marginal improvement and practical requirements instead of raw fit alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-23": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>23. Q1-23 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Model A has AIC 110 and model B has AIC 116 on comparable data. Which is preferred by AIC?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AIC is minimized. Its score balances likelihood with a parameter penalty; a lower score is relative support rather than proof of true structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-24": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>24. Q1-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Income ranges in millions while a credit measure ranges in hundreds. Which preparations can make distances more comparable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Standardize each feature using training statistics; B. Evaluate whether feature weights reflect the intended notion of similarity; D. Scale each feature using its training range</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Scaling addresses units, and meaningful weighting addresses substantive importance. Increasing the already large scale worsens numerical dominance without justification.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q1-25": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>25. Q1-25 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A large tree improves training accuracy by isolating one observation in a leaf. What conclusions are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Validation or pruning can assess whether the split helps; C. That split is a warning sign for overfitting; D. The training improvement alone does not prove better generalization</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A singleton leaf can fit noise. The sample quiz treats this as an overfitting concern; an extra feature or smaller training error is insufficient evidence of value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q1-26": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>26. Q1-26 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two observations differ by 6 in one feature and 8 in another. What is their Euclidean distance?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 10</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Euclidean distance is sqrt(6 squared+8 squared)=10. Fourteen is Manhattan distance, eight is the infinity norm, and 100 is squared Euclidean distance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-27": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>27. Q1-27 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A single extreme production record is found. Which actions are justified before deciding whether to exclude it?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assess whether the intended model should cover that condition; C. Investigate sensor or transcription problems; D. Check whether an unusual real operating condition occurred</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Outlier handling depends on validity and relevance. Removing a meaningful adverse event simply to improve fit can produce unrealistic predictions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-28": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>28. Q1-28 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An investor wants a forecast of tomorrow&#x27;s conditional return variance. Which method is most directly designed for this?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. GARCH</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> GARCH models conditional variance using variance and squared-shock history. A separate mean model can describe expected return.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — GARCH models variance, not the mean response:</strong> Separate the conditional mean from conditional variance. In a GARCH(1,1) update, both the previous squared shock and previous variance matter. Squaring removes shock direction. A variance forecast can stay large after a small shock when past variance is large.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q1-29": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>29. Q1-29 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In an additive regression, income is predicted as 20,000+500*age+2,000*education. What does the age coefficient mean?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. One more year of age is associated with $500 higher predicted income holding education fixed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The coefficient is a conditional unit change in the modeled response. It is neither a causal proof, a probability increment, nor a goodness-of-fit percentage.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-30": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>30. Q1-30 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Loan records contain ZIP codes written as five-digit numbers. What is their usual data type?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Categorical</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A numerical representation does not create magnitude or meaningful arithmetic. ZIP codes identify areas; differences between codes are not geographic distances.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-31": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>31. Q1-31 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which regressions are linear in their fitted coefficients?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. log(y)=b0+b1*x; B. y=b0+b1*x+b2*x squared; D. y=b0+b1*x1+b2*x1*x2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Transforming predictors or the response does not destroy linearity in coefficients when coefficients multiply fixed columns. A coefficient inside an exponential makes the last form nonlinear in that coefficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-32": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>32. Q1-32 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-means cluster contains coordinates (0,0), (2,4), and (4,2). What is its updated center?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. (2,2)</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Average each coordinate: both means equal two. The centroid need not coincide with one of the original observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-33": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>33. Q1-33 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A retailer operating five days weekly assigns every second record to validation in a fixed five-record cycle. What is the central concern?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The split may isolate a particular weekday</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Rotation can synchronize with seasonality and omit weekdays from training. Representative allocation within relevant groups is preferable to unexamined systematic rotation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-34": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>34. Q1-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which feature distinguishes random forests from bagged trees that consider every predictor at each split?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A fresh random subset of candidate predictors at each split</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Both methods can bootstrap observations. Random candidate-feature subsets help diversify trees and reduce correlated prediction errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-35": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>35. Q1-35 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A temperature is normal in summer but extremely unusual for its recorded winter date. Which outlier type best describes it?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Contextual</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The abnormality depends on seasonal context. A value need not be globally extreme to be unusual at a particular time.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-36": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>36. Q1-36 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which interpretations of ARIMA(2,1,3) are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Two autoregressive lags are included; B. Three lagged innovations are included; C. The series is differenced once</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The order tuple is p,d,q: lagged values, differencing order, lagged errors. These are distinct from clustering or seasonal cycle length.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q1-37": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>37. Q1-37 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A three-class k-NN rule sees five neighbors: red, red, blue, green, blue. What additional detail is needed to return a unique class?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A tie-breaking rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Red and blue each have two votes. Multi-class voting can tie even with odd k; the algorithm must specify how to resolve it.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-38": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>38. Q1-38 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about a p-value of .01 for a coefficient are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It can indicate evidence against a zero-coefficient null; D. It is computed assuming the tested null and statistical model</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A p-value is a tail probability under a null, not a posterior probability of that null. Statistical evidence and practical magnitude are different questions. The .01 describes how surprising the observed statistic, or a more extreme one, would be under the specified null and assumptions. It is not a 99% probability that the coefficient is real, does not measure the size of the effect, and does not identify a causal effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-39": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>39. Q1-39 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> PCA eigenvalues are 8, 3, and 1. What fraction of predictor variance is retained by the first two components?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 11/12</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sum retained eigenvalues and divide by total: (8+3)/(8+3+1)=11/12. This measures predictor variance, not response variance explained by a supervised model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-40": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>40. Q1-40 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements describe standard Euclidean k-means correctly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It uses no known response labels; C. Multiple initializations can produce different final solutions; D. It repeatedly assigns points and updates means</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The alternating procedure is a fast heuristic and can stop at local optima. Repeated starts improve the opportunity to find a better solution, not a proof of global optimality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-41": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>41. Q1-41 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> After CV chooses a model specification, which actions are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Evaluate the refitted model on reserved test data; B. Refit the chosen specification on all non-test data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cross-validation selects a specification. The lecture recommends a fresh fit on the available non-test data rather than selecting or averaging the intermediate fitted coefficients.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-42": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>42. Q1-42 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For upper CUSUM, previous S=4, x=15, mu=10, C=2. What is the new S?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 7</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> S=max(0,4+15-10-2)=7. The allowance is subtracted before applying the reset to zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-43": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>43. Q1-43 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A multiplicative seasonal factor for early-batch parts is .8 and the response is operating hours before failure. What conclusion is supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Early-batch parts have lower expected operating hours than the adjusted baseline</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A factor below one reduces expected response by a proportion. It suggests earlier failure but does not establish a mechanism or an additive 80-hour difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-44": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>44. Q1-44 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> From 1,000 observations, 200 are reserved for testing. Four-fold CV is applied to the other 800. How many observations train each CV fit?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 600</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each fold contains 200 observations, so each fit uses three folds, or 600. The reserved test observations participate in none of these fits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-45": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>45. Q1-45 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Ten candidate models are fit on training data and the best validation performer is chosen. Which dataset should provide the final reported estimate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. An untouched test set</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Selection can capitalize on validation randomness. A test set independent of fitting and selection estimates performance of the selected procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-46": {"key": "0; 1", "html": "<h2>46. Q1-46 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> After subtracting a feature&#x27;s mean and dividing by its standard deviation, the transformed feature has mean ____ and standard deviation ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0; 1</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> This is standardization. It changes location and scale but does not guarantee a normal distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-47": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>47. Q1-47 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A test set remains an independent final evaluation set even if its labels were used to choose the best threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Threshold selection uses test outcomes and turns that dataset into selection data. A fresh independent evaluation is then needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-48": {"key": "Voronoi", "html": "<h2>48. Q1-48 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The regions consisting of all points nearest to each fixed cluster center form a ____ diagram.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> Voronoi</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each region contains points closest to one center under the chosen distance metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-49": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>49. Q1-49 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For ordinary Holt forecasting, the h-step-ahead forecast is current level plus h times current trend.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The future baseline extends the current estimated per-period trend across h periods.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-50": {"key": "0.75; 75%", "html": "<h2>50. Q1-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For TP=36 and FP=12, precision is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.75; 75%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Precision is TP/(TP+FP)=36/48=.75; it answers how many positive predictions are correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q2-01": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>1. Q2-01 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which reasons can justify kernel SVM rather than a strictly linear separator?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The feature-label relationship has a curved boundary; C. A nonlinear separator may fit patterns missed by a straight hyperplane; D. Suitable kernel settings improve validation performance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Kernels expand the available boundary shapes. Their usefulness must be evaluated; more flexible boundaries do not establish causality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-02": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>2. Q2-02 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For one-dimensional points 1, 3, and 8, what is WSS about their mean 4?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 26</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Squared deviations are 9,1,16, totaling 26. WSS uses squares; the sum of absolute deviations is a different objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-03": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>3. Q2-03 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Daily sales have a seven-day cycle. When updating Tuesday&#x27;s multiplicative seasonal pattern, which stored factor is relevant?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The most recent previous Tuesday&#x27;s factor</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lag t-L points to the same position in the last cycle. Using t-1 would confound neighboring days with the same seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-04": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>4. Q2-04 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about bootstrap samples used for forests are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Sampling is with replacement; B. Some training records can be absent from a tree&#x27;s sample; C. A record can appear more than once in one tree&#x27;s sample</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Drawing n times from n records with replacement allows duplicates and omissions. This creates different training samples without collecting new observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-05": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>5. Q2-05 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish additive from multiplicative seasonality?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Multiplicative seasonality represents a proportion of level; B. A factor of 1 indicates no multiplicative seasonal change; D. Additive seasonality represents a unit offset</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiplicative updates divide by seasonal factors, so zeros are problematic. Additive and proportional effects describe different relationships to the level.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-06": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>6. Q2-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which ARIMA specification contains only an autoregressive component, with neither differencing nor lagged innovations?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. ARIMA(3,0,0)</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first position counts AR lags, the second differencing, and the third MA lags.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q2-07": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>7. Q2-07 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A flexible spline has training MSE zero and much higher held-out MSE than a straight-line model. What is the best interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The spline may fit dataset-specific noise</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Exact interpolation can overfit. Independent predictive performance is more informative than training perfection.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-08": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>8. Q2-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With alpha=0 in single smoothing, what happens to the level after initialization?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It stays at the initial level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The recurrence gives level_t=level_(t-1). This is an extreme of maximal inertia, not an adaptive forecast.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-09": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>9. Q2-09 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Three regression trees predict 8, 10, and 15 for one case. What is the forest&#x27;s mean prediction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 11</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Average the responses: (8+10+15)/3=11. A mode is appropriate to class voting, not these numeric predictions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-10": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>10. Q2-10 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For y=5+2*x1+3*x2+4*x1*x2, what is the marginal effect of x1 when x2=2?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 10</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The derivative with respect to x1 is 2+4*x2. At x2=2, it is ten; the interaction changes the effect across x2 values.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-11": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>11. Q2-11 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A restaurant first predicts next week&#x27;s demand, then chooses purchase quantities under spoilage and shortage costs. What is the role of the demand forecast?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A predictive input to a prescriptive decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Expected demand supports an inventory decision, but quantities also depend on costs, constraints, and uncertainty. Prediction and prescription are connected stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q2-12": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>12. Q2-12 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A Holt model has current level 100 and trend -3. What is its four-step-ahead forecast?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 88</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The forecast is 100+4*(-3)=88. The horizon multiplies the estimated per-period trend.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-13": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>13. Q2-13 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A new point is equally far from two learned centers. What is needed for deterministic cluster assignment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A tie policy</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Nearest-center assignment can tie. A documented tie policy yields reproducible assignment without changing the unsupervised training task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-14": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>14. Q2-14 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In GARCH(1,1), h_t=2+.1*epsilon_(t-1) squared+.8*h_(t-1). If the squared shock is 9 and previous variance is 4, what is h_t?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 6.1</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Compute 2+.1*9+.8*4=6.1. Variance updates use squared shocks, not the signed shock itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — GARCH models variance, not the mean response:</strong> Separate the conditional mean from conditional variance. In a GARCH(1,1) update, both the previous squared shock and previous variance matter. Squaring removes shock direction. A variance forecast can stay large after a small shock when past variance is large.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q2-15": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>15. Q2-15 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Seven nearest neighbors have labels A,A,A,B,B,C,C. What does unweighted plurality voting predict?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A appears most often, with three of seven votes. Multi-class classification requires the largest count, not an absolute majority unless a special abstention policy is specified.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-16": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>16. Q2-16 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-means cluster centers are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. They need not be observed data points; C. An extreme point can pull a center; D. They are coordinatewise means under the standard objective</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Centers summarize feature values learned from memberships. They are not supervised labels and may lie between observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-17": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>17. Q2-17 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two movements need 5 seconds horizontally and 12 seconds vertically and occur simultaneously. Which completion-time norm applies?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. L-infinity, giving 12 seconds</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> When both begin together, completion waits for the slower movement. The maximum time is 12 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-18": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>18. Q2-18 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A dataset contains transaction amount, payment method, and whether repayment occurred. Which column is the response in a model predicting repayment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Whether repayment occurred</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The response is the observed outcome to be predicted; amounts and methods can be predictors. Column roles depend on the task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-19": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>19. Q2-19 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> When comparing repeated k-means starts at the same k, which practices make sense?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Ensure the distance and scaling are consistent across runs; B. Recognize that different starts can converge to the same solution; D. Keep the solution with lower WSS on the same prepared data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cluster labels are arbitrary; changing label names does not change fit. Compare a common objective under the same representation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-20": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>20. Q2-20 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which assumptions yield the equivalence between Gaussian maximum likelihood and least squares for coefficients?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Independent errors; B. Common error variance; D. Normally distributed errors with mean zero</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Under iid zero-mean Gaussian errors, maximizing likelihood amounts to minimizing SSE. Unequal known variances instead lead to a weighted objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-21": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>21. Q2-21 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish underfitting from overfitting?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Validation can help choose an appropriate flexibility level; C. An overly flexible form may fit random structure; D. An overly restrictive form may miss real structure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Underfitting misses signal and overfitting follows noise. Held-out evaluation helps assess the tradeoff rather than maximizing training fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-22": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>22. Q2-22 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Summer ends later in a local dataset. What additional information is needed before claiming that summer duration increased?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. When summer began under a consistent definition</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Duration depends on both start and end. A narrow local analysis also cannot settle broader climate conclusions by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-23": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>23. Q2-23 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A training feature has minimum 20 and maximum 60. What is the min-max scaled value of 30?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. .25</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Subtract 20 and divide by 40: (30-20)/(60-20)=.25. Use the training range consistently for later observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q2-24": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>24. Q2-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which points can have zero hinge loss in a normalized linear SVM?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A point with y*f=1; D. A point with y*f=2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Hinge loss max(0,1-y*f) vanishes on or beyond the correct margin. Correct sign alone is insufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-25": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>25. Q2-25 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Software minimizes .5*norm(w) squared+C*sum(hinge loss). What does increasing software C emphasize?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Reducing training hinge loss</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Here C multiplies the loss. Its direction differs from a lecture lambda that multiplies the norm penalty. C multiplies the loss term here. Increasing it makes violations more expensive relative to the norm penalty. Under equivalent sum-loss conventions, dividing the objective by C gives a norm coefficient 1/(2*C), illustrating the opposite direction to lambda in the other form.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-26": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>26. Q2-26 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For w=(3,4), what is the full hard-margin SVM width under boundaries f=+1 and f=-1?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 0.4</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The coefficient norm is five, so full width is 2/5=.4. Confusing norm with margin reverses the geometry.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-27": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>27. Q2-27 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> What information is needed to tune change-detection settings sensibly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Costs of false alarms; C. Representative behavior before and after changes; D. Costs of detection delay</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sensitivity choices depend on operational tradeoffs and representative data. Unnecessary stops or repeated alarms can impose substantial costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-28": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>28. Q2-28 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which steps correctly detrend a response for factor-based modeling?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Add back the relevant trend when recovering original-scale predictions; B. Estimate an appropriate time trend on the permitted training data; C. Subtract that fitted trend from observed responses</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Detrending removes the selected trend component. Seasonality and other dependence may remain, and original-scale forecasts need the reverse adjustment.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-29": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>29. Q2-29 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about increasing regression complexity are correct for nested least-squares fits with an intercept on the same training data?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Adjusted R-squared may decrease; C. Training R-squared cannot decrease; D. Training SSE cannot increase</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> More columns expand the possible fitted responses, so training fit cannot worsen. A complexity-adjusted metric or independent predictive score can worsen.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-30": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>30. Q2-30 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A feature is standardized to z=(x-50)/10. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. x=70 maps to z=2; D. x=30 maps to z=-2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Standardization expresses distance from the mean in standard-deviation units. It neither bounds the values nor changes the distribution into a Gaussian shape.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q2-31": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>31. Q2-31 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A tree root asks age&lt;30. Its older branch asks income&lt;50,000. For age=40 and income=60,000, which path is taken?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Older, then higher-income</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Follow the actual predicates sequentially: age&lt;30 is false and income&lt;50,000 is false. Boundary inequalities must be read exactly as written.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q2-32": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>32. Q2-32 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Centered and standardized predictors x1,x2 have correlation .98. What is usually expected from their PCA eigenvalues?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. One much larger eigenvalue and one much smaller eigenvalue</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Strong correlation concentrates standardized predictor variation along a shared direction. PCA uses no response, and standardization does not remove feature correlation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-33": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>33. Q2-33 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A logistic slope equals log(2), with no interaction. What does a one-unit predictor increase do holding other predictors fixed?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Doubles the odds</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Exponentiating the slope gives the odds multiplier exp(log(2))=2. Probability changes depend on its starting value. Odds become 2 times their previous value, because exp(log(2))=2. For example, p=.20 means odds .25; doubling gives odds .50 and p=.50/1.50=1/3, not .40. The probability change depends on the starting probability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Logistic probabilities versus odds:</strong> Convert eta to p=1/(1+exp(-eta)). Odds=p/(1-p); a one-unit predictor change multiplies odds by exp(beta) in an additive logistic model. It does not multiply probability by the same factor. A probability model and a classification threshold are separate choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-34": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>34. Q2-34 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A series has values 10, 13, and 18. What is the second difference at the third observation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> First differences are 3 and 5, so the second difference is 5-3=2. It is not the two-period difference 18-10.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q2-35": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>35. Q2-35 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forecasting model will use data through Friday to predict Saturday. Which evaluation design best mirrors deployment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Train on earlier periods and validate on later periods</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A chronological evaluation reproduces what information will exist at forecast time. Random splits can allow future patterns or preprocessing information into training.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Forecast validation without future information:</strong> Use training windows that precede their forecast targets. Distinguish forecast errors from in-sample residuals. A rolling origin evaluates forecasts at repeated historical cutoffs, using only information available at each cutoff.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/tscv.html\">Forecasting textbook: time-series validation</a></p>"}, "Q2-36": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>36. Q2-36 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about large residuals are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. They can indicate a missing predictor or wrong model form; C. They merit investigation in the intended application; D. They can indicate data errors</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Residuals measure discrepancy from the fitted model, which itself may be incomplete or misspecified. They are evidence to examine rather than proof of bad data.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-37": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>37. Q2-37 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which preprocessing practices avoid leakage in cross-validation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Estimate feature means on each fold&#x27;s training portion; C. Apply those fitted means to the held-out fold; D. Select predictors using only the fold&#x27;s training outcomes</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Preprocessing and feature selection are parts of fitting. Letting held-out observations influence them makes validation more optimistic.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-38": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>38. Q2-38 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions follow from retaining all orthonormal PCA components of a feature space?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The representation changes coordinates; C. No information is discarded solely by the full rotation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA only reduces dimensionality when components are omitted. A complete orthogonal transformation preserves the available feature information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-39": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>39. Q2-39 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For observed responses 2, 4, 6 and predictions 3, 3, 5, what is SSE?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Residuals are -1,1,1; their squares sum to three. Signed residuals can cancel, which is why the objective does not simply sum them.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-40": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>40. Q2-40 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ARIMA&#x27;s moving-average component are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its order q counts innovation lags; B. It can coexist with an autoregressive component; C. It uses past innovations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The MA terminology here refers to errors entering the time-series equation. It is not a sliding arithmetic average of observed levels. ARIMA MA uses lagged forecast innovations or shocks in a stochastic model. A rolling average of the last q observed responses is a different operation. The index q is the number of innovation lags, not the width of a rolling-data average.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q2-41": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>41. Q2-41 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Five-fold validation MSE values are 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8 for equal-sized folds. What is their average?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 6</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The mean is (4+5+6+7+8)/5=6. Equal fold sizes make an unweighted mean appropriate here.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-42": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>42. Q2-42 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For upper CUSUM, previous S=3, x=7, mu=10, C=1. What is the new statistic?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 0</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The raw sum is 3+7-10-1=-1, so reset to zero. Carrying negative debt would delay detection of a later increase.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-43": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>43. Q2-43 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about inference and prediction are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A less interpretable method may be useful when predictive accuracy is the priority; D. An interpretable model can help explain modeled associations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Model complexity can reduce interpretability and may overfit. Neither flexibility nor accurate prediction guarantees causal explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q2-44": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>44. Q2-44 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A heartbeat sequence lacks one expected beat, but none of the observed amplitudes is unusual. Which representation can expose a point outlier?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Time between consecutive beats</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A missed beat creates an unusually long interbeat interval. Transforming the representation can reveal the anomaly as an extreme individual value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-45": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>45. Q2-45 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression has SSE=40 and SST=100. What is ordinary R-squared?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. .60</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> R-squared=1-SSE/SST=1-.4=.6. It measures fit relative to a mean-response baseline.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-46": {"key": "14", "html": "<h2>46. Q2-46 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For coordinate differences of 6 and 8, Manhattan distance is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 14</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> L1 adds absolute differences: 6+8=14.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-47": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>47. Q2-47 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If removing an outlier improves fit, that alone proves removal is appropriate for the intended application.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Improved fit is not evidence that the record is invalid or irrelevant. Investigate origin and whether the target use must represent rare events.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-48": {"key": "246", "html": "<h2>48. Q2-48 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With level=200, trend=5, and the next multiplicative seasonal factor=1.2, the one-step forecast is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 246</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiply the projected baseline by the seasonal factor: (200+5)*1.2=246.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-49": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>49. Q2-49 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A low p-value is sufficient evidence that changing the predictor will cause the estimated change in the response.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A low p-value can support a modeled association. Confounding, reverse causation, and design limitations still matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-50": {"key": "0.75; 75%", "html": "<h2>50. Q2-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If a classification leaf has 9 positive and 3 negative training cases, its empirical positive fraction is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.75; 75%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fraction is 9/(9+3)=.75. The majority class is positive, while .75 is a probability estimate rather than a causal claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-01": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>1. Q3-01 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which split designs can address nonrepresentative subsets?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Stratify by important classes when appropriate; B. Check whether systematic rotation aligns with periodic structure; C. Consider relevant regimes or groups</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Randomization is useful but does not guarantee rare-case coverage. Design should match the population, dependencies, and use case.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-02": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>2. Q3-02 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about confidence and prediction intervals are correct in standard regression?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. An individual prediction interval also includes new-observation noise; C. An individual prediction interval is generally wider at the same confidence level; D. A mean-response confidence interval concerns the conditional mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Predicting a new individual includes uncertainty in the estimated mean plus irreducible observation variability. This book-supported distinction helps interpret uncertainty correctly. A mean-response interval accounts for uncertainty in estimating the conditional mean. An individual prediction interval also accounts for the new observation&#x27;s random error and is therefore wider at the same predictor values and confidence level under the standard model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-03": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>3. Q3-03 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which property most directly motivates ARIMA differencing?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The observed series may have a nonstationary level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Differencing can yield a more stable series suitable for ARMA modeling. It is not a classification or clustering operation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q3-04": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>4. Q3-04 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two orthogonal PCA loadings produce a high-variance component unrelated to outcome and a low-variance component that separates classes. What does PCA alone recommend retaining first?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The high-variance component</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ordinary PCA ranks predictor variation without using labels. The example shows why dimension reduction can discard useful supervised information. PCA optimizes variance in X and has no response information with which to recognize the discriminative low-variance direction. Choosing components solely by explained predictor variance can therefore remove signal valuable for classification.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-05": {"key": "ABCD", "html": "<h2>5. Q3-05 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which interpretations of linear SVM coefficients require care?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assuming a coefficient describes a causal effect; B. Comparing magnitudes before making feature scales comparable; C. Recognizing that a zero coefficient removes that feature from this fitted score; D. Declaring a small coefficient globally irrelevant despite correlated features</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Units, correlation, and task context affect coefficient interpretation. Zero contribution in this fitted linear score is precise; global irrelevance and causality are broader unsupported claims.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-06": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>6. Q3-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A baseline smoothing update uses alpha=.8, observation=50, previous level=100. What is the new level?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 60</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The weighted average is .8*50+.2*100=60. High alpha makes the level move strongly toward the latest observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-07": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>7. Q3-07 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which changes alter the substantive clustering problem?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Change the distance metric; B. Switch from miles to dollars on one dimension without scaling policy; D. Analyze day and night separately when their distributions differ</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Representation, geometry, and segmentation can change similarity. Pure label renaming does not change the partition.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-08": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>8. Q3-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which initializations are the simple defaults described in the lectures?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Initial multiplicative seasonal factors equal one; B. First level equals the first observation; D. Initial trend equals zero</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Zero denotes no initial trend; one denotes no initial multiplicative effect. Zero seasonal divisors would invalidate the ratio updates. These are the simple lecture starting choices, not universal optimal initial estimates. More sophisticated software may estimate initial components. A factor of one means neutral multiplicative seasonality; zero would make deseasonalization undefined.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-09": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>9. Q3-09 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A lower-direction monitor uses Sminus=max(0,Sminus_previous+mu-x-C). With previous=2, mu=20, x=16, C=1, what is the new value?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 5</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The positive-valued lower statistic accumulates evidence of decreases: 2+20-16-1=5. This convention signals when its positive threshold is exceeded.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-10": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>10. Q3-10 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-NN model has good training behavior but poor validation performance after adding many irrelevant dimensions. What is a sensible next step?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Evaluate feature selection or meaningful weighting inside validation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Irrelevant dimensions can distort proximity and weaken neighborhoods. Feature selection and weighting should be evaluated without contaminating the final test.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-11": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>11. Q3-11 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A binary leaf contains equal positive and negative proportions. What is its Gini impurity sum p_k*(1-p_k)?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. .5</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each class contributes .5*.5=.25; the sum is .5. A pure leaf has zero impurity.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-12": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>12. Q3-12 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In a forest, one strong predictor repeatedly dominates splits when all features are considered. Why can candidate-feature subsampling help?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It permits alternative splits and reduces tree correlation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Diversification can make ensemble averaging more effective by weakening shared errors. Individual trees can become weaker while the ensemble improves.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-13": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>13. Q3-13 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Twenty SVM settings and ten neighbor settings are compared by CV. What is selected at the end of CV?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A modeling specification and tuning settings</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CV compares procedures/settings. Fit the chosen specification again using all permitted non-test data before final evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-14": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>14. Q3-14 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Model A has log-likelihood -40 and K=3 parameters. What is AIC using -2*logL+2*K?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 86</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AIC=-2*(-40)+2*3=80+6=86. The log-likelihood term and complexity penalty have different roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-15": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>15. Q3-15 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-means elbow curve has WSS values 400, 210, 110, 98, 90 at k=1..5. Which k is the most plausible elbow starting point?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Large improvements occur through k=3, followed by much smaller reductions. Operational constraints can still justify a different choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-16": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>16. Q3-16 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about eigenvectors used in PCA are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. For X transpose X, eigenvalues are nonnegative; B. They satisfy A*v=lambda*v for nonzero v; C. Loading directions can be chosen orthogonal</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> X transpose X is symmetric positive semidefinite. That special structure supports orthogonal directions and nonnegative eigenvalues; arbitrary matrices do not share all those properties.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-17": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>17. Q3-17 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A rare toxic class is costly to miss. Which classification adjustments can be appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Evaluate the resulting false alarms and total cost; B. Use a lower toxic-probability threshold if miss costs dominate; D. Increase the penalty for missing toxic cases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Unequal costs justify asymmetric decisions. A threshold is a decision policy, not a mathematical requirement of the probability model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-18": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>18. Q3-18 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression estimates an effect of .001 dollars per unit with p&lt;.001. Which conclusion is most appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. There may be statistical evidence for a very small practical effect</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The scale of the coefficient determines practical magnitude. A small p-value does not establish importance or causation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-19": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>19. Q3-19 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A distant observation forms a legitimate small customer segment. Why might blindly removing it be harmful?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It can erase a real segment important to the business</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Rare but relevant groups may carry value. The appropriate response depends on the target population and intended decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-20": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>20. Q3-20 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which measures can grow classification trees in the book&#x27;s treatment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Entropy; B. Gini impurity; C. Class proportions used within those impurity measures</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Gini and entropy measure class mixture and respond to node purity. Numeric averages of arbitrary class codes need not represent meaningful categorical impurity.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-21": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>21. Q3-21 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements describe the advanced regression introductions?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Splines join polynomial pieces at knots; C. Bayesian regression updates priors using data; D. Poisson regression models count means as functions of features</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> k-NN regression averages numerical responses; voting is its classification version. The other descriptions match the introduced method families.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Introduced regression families:</strong> Know the lecture-level distinctions: Poisson for count means, Bayesian updating of a parameter distribution, splines as joined polynomial pieces, and neighbor regression as local averaging. Use companion lectures to clarify a distinction rather than treating every advanced implementation detail as required scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression, nonlinear-model, and Bayesian companion lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-22": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>22. Q3-22 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A separator is horizontal at x2=5, with x1 on the horizontal axis. Which feature controls the classification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. x2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Crossing a horizontal line requires changing the vertical coordinate x2. The rule is insensitive to x1 in this model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-23": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>23. Q3-23 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A constant training feature equals 12 in every record. What issue arises when standardizing it?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its standard deviation is zero</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Dividing by zero is undefined. Remove the constant feature or use an explicit safe convention rather than applying the formula blindly.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q3-24": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>24. Q3-24 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A transport firm estimates how deliveries behaved last year, rather than selecting routes or predicting next month. What is the main question type?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Descriptive</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Descriptive analytics characterizes observed behavior. The same project may later use those findings for predictions or decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q3-25": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>25. Q3-25 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which quantities enter the lecture&#x27;s multiplicative Holt-Winters level update?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A level smoothing coefficient alpha; B. Previous level plus previous trend; C. Current observation divided by the previous same-phase seasonal factor</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The update blends a deseasonalized current observation with the previous projected baseline. A future observed response is not available.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-26": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>26. Q3-26 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An SVM separator is f(x)=2*x1-3*x2+6. For x1=0,x2=1, what class does the sign rule predict?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Positive</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> f=0-3+6=3, which is positive. This is a class decision, not a probability of three.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-27": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>27. Q3-27 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For rare-event probability p=.08, false-positive cost=$10 and false-negative cost=$200, with correct decisions free, what minimizes expected cost for this case?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Predict positive</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Positive-decision expected cost is .92*10=$9.20; negative-decision cost is .08*200=$16. Positive is cheaper under the stated costs and calibrated probability. Predicting positive costs (1-.08)*10=$9.20 in expectation. Predicting negative costs .08*200=$16. The positive action is cheaper even though infection is less likely than non-infection. The implied threshold is 10/210, about .0476.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-28": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>28. Q3-28 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A weekly production response is observed once per week and repeats annually, ignoring irregular week counts for this question. What cycle length is a natural approximation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 52 observations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The cycle length is measured in observations, not raw calendar days. A year contains approximately 52 weekly observations under this stated simplification.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-29": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>29. Q3-29 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two candidate centers are (0,0) and (8,0). Under Euclidean distance, which center receives new point (3,2)?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. (0,0)</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Squared distances are 13 and 29, so the first center is nearer. New-point assignment uses the learned centers.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-30": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>30. Q3-30 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model assigns scores .8,.6 to two actual positives and .7,.1 to two negatives. With no ties, what is AUC?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. .75</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Of four positive-negative pairs, .8 beats both negatives and .6 beats only .1: three concordant pairs out of four. There are four positive-negative pairs: .8 beats .7 and .1, while .6 beats .1 but loses to .7. Three of four are ranked correctly, giving AUC=.75. This calculation does not require choosing a classification threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q3-31": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>31. Q3-31 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The same patient&#x27;s measurements appear in both training and validation, although deployment targets new patients. What is the concern?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Validation can benefit from patient-specific overlap</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Related observations can leak individual patterns across subsets. Grouped splitting by patient better assesses generalization to previously unseen patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-32": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>32. Q3-32 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about stationarity are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its variance does not systematically change over time; B. Its lag covariance depends on lag rather than calendar position; C. A weakly stationary series has a constant mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Stationarity restricts temporal distributional structure. One difference can help some trends but is not a universal cure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q3-33": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>33. Q3-33 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For single smoothing with alpha=.2, what is the explicit weight on the immediately previous observation in the expanded recursion, away from initialization?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. .16</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The previous observation&#x27;s weight is alpha*(1-alpha)=.2*.8=.16. More distant weights multiply by additional powers of .8.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-34": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>34. Q3-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are valid reasons for pruning or limiting a tree?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Children would have too few observations; B. A split&#x27;s training improvement does not persist on validation; C. Complexity encourages fitting noise</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Pruning and minimum-size requirements control model flexibility. Tree depth can improve training fit while harming generalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-35": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>35. Q3-35 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which circumstances can generate a genuine outlying record?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A one-time publicity event affecting sales; C. A rare weather-related delivery disruption; D. Natural tail variation in a large sample</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first three can be real outcomes. A known transcription error is a measurement problem, even if the wrongly recorded value looks plausible.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-36": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>36. Q3-36 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Upper CUSUM is reset when its raw cumulative value becomes negative. What purpose does this serve?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It avoids carrying downward evidence that delays a later upward alarm</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Negative accumulated deviations do not help identify future upward shifts. Resetting improves responsiveness but does not eliminate randomness or false alarms.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-37": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>37. Q3-37 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A fitted mean forecast is stable but residual volatility rises and falls in clusters. What additional component may model that behavior?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A conditional variance model such as GARCH</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Time-varying residual variance is different from time-varying mean. GARCH targets the conditional variance process.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — GARCH models variance, not the mean response:</strong> Separate the conditional mean from conditional variance. In a GARCH(1,1) update, both the previous squared shock and previous variance matter. Squaring removes shock direction. A variance forecast can stay large after a small shock when past variance is large.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q3-38": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>38. Q3-38 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which quantities should remain distinct in a CUSUM analysis?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Baseline expectation mu; B. Per-observation allowance C; D. Cumulative alarm threshold T</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Mu sets the reference level, C discounts small deviations, and T specifies evidence required to alarm. They play different roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-39": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>39. Q3-39 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> When comparing AIC or BIC scores, which precautions matter?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Use comparable observations and likelihood definitions; C. Prefer smaller values for the stated criteria; D. Count parameters consistently</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Likelihood scale and parameter counting affect the comparison. Transformations may require accounting for the changed density scale rather than comparing arbitrary reported numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-40": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>40. Q3-40 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A Box-Cox transformation is applied with lambda=1 to y=9. What transformed value follows the standard definition?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 8</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> For nonzero lambda, (y^lambda-1)/lambda gives (9-1)/1=8. Lambda zero is the log limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-41": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>41. Q3-41 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish clustering from classification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A new point can be assigned to a discovered cluster without known response; C. Clustering discovers groups from feature similarity; D. Classification learns from known response categories</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering groups are algorithmic outputs, not preobserved supervised answers or proven causal types. Their practical meaning must be investigated.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-42": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>42. Q3-42 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which concerns should guide the choice between an interpretable regression and a forest?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Need to explain individual modeled relationships; B. Whether decision-makers can use the output; C. Held-out predictive performance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Model choice depends on intended use and evidence. Complexity and branding do not establish suitability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q3-43": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>43. Q3-43 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A binary predictor is one for premium members and zero otherwise. In an additive model its coefficient is 7. What is its interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Premium membership is associated with a seven-unit predicted response difference holding other predictors fixed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A dummy coefficient compares the indicated group with its reference under the model. Interactions can change this comparison across other feature values.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-44": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>44. Q3-44 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about training and validation randomness are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A fitted model can capture accidental training patterns; C. A validation winner can benefit from accidental validation patterns; D. A separate test set reduces selection-related optimism</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Every finite sample contains randomness. Independent testing reduces a particular selection bias but does not make a score exact or deterministic.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-45": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>45. Q3-45 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A response is detrended as r=y-(10+2*t). At t=5 a model predicts r=3. What original-scale response is predicted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 23</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Restore the fitted trend: 3+(10+2*5)=23. Reporting the residual-scale prediction as the original response would omit the baseline movement.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-46": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>46. Q3-46 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A linear SVM score of 2.3 is automatically a predicted probability of 2.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An SVM score is a signed decision function, not automatically a calibrated probability. A probability cannot exceed one.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-47": {"key": "1; one", "html": "<h2>47. Q3-47 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In six-fold CV, each non-test observation serves as a validation observation ____ time(s).</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 1; one</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each fold is held out once, so every observation is validated once and used to train the other five fits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-48": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>48. Q3-48 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A larger CUSUM threshold usually requires more accumulated evidence before an alarm.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Increasing the threshold typically delays detection and reduces false alarms, all other settings equal.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-49": {"key": "3", "html": "<h2>49. Q3-49 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The regression residual for observed y=12 and fitted y=9 is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Residuals conventionally equal observed minus fitted, so 12-9=3.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-50": {"key": "80", "html": "<h2>50. Q3-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With actual positive prevalence .1, sensitivity .8, and specificity .9, expected TP per 1,000 observations is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 80</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> There are 100 expected actual positives, and .8 of them are correctly detected, giving 80.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q4-01": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>1. Q4-01 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about likelihood are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. For continuous data it uses densities; B. It is a function of parameters for the observed data; D. It is not generally a probability distribution over parameter values</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Continuous densities can exceed one and do not behave like probabilities assigned to parameter hypotheses. Bayesian posteriors require priors and normalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-02": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>2. Q4-02 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For centered/scaled predictors, a regression on one component has slope b=4 and loadings (.5,.5). What are implied slopes in those centered/scaled coordinates?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. (2,2)</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiply loading weights by the component regression coefficient: each is .5*4=2. Additional adjustments restore raw-unit slopes and the intercept if features were standardized.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-03": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>3. Q4-03 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A PCA loading for one centered feature is .6 and the other is .8. For centered x=(5,0), what is its component score?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The score is the dot product with the loading direction: .6*5+.8*0=3.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-04": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>4. Q4-04 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team tries many CUSUM settings until the conclusion matches its prior political belief and reports only that result. What is the central failure?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. It substitutes the desired conclusion for faithful reporting of analysis</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture&#x27;s ethics point is to report defensible analyses honestly, including sensitivity and limits. Tuning to force a preferred claim undermines that responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-05": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>5. Q4-05 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In hard-margin SVM, why are response labels encoded -1 and +1 in the lecture derivation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Both class requirements combine as y*f&gt;=1</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiplying the signed score by the signed label expresses the correct side for either class using one inequality. This encoding is an algebraic convenience, not a performance guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-06": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>6. Q4-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A single-smoothed series has current level 73. What are its point forecasts one and five periods ahead?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Both 73</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Single smoothing extrapolates a constant level. Prediction uncertainty can still grow with horizon despite identical point forecasts.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-07": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>7. Q4-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With positive defined as default, increasing a fixed logistic classification threshold from .2 to .7 usually does what?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Reduces both true-positive rate and false-positive rate</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Fewer cases meet a higher threshold. Under a fixed score distribution, positive classifications decrease, though discrete scores can create unchanged intervals.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q4-08": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>8. Q4-08 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two predictors are nearly redundant. What book-supported issue can arise in regression coefficients?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Large uncertainty in separate slopes even when combined predictions are useful</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Collinearity makes it hard to separate conditional contributions and can inflate coefficient standard errors. It does not automatically eliminate predictive value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Residual plots and regression assumptions:</strong> Separate nonlinearity, unequal residual spread, dependence, and unusually influential observations. A curved pattern suggests a mean-model problem; changing spread suggests a variance problem. Assumptions concern errors conditional on predictors rather than requiring every predictor to be normally distributed.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression diagnostics lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-09": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>9. Q4-09 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Holt trend update uses beta=.4, new level=30, old level=25, old trend=2. What is the new trend?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 3.2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Blend observed level change and old trend: .4*(30-25)+.6*2=2+1.2=3.2.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-10": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>10. Q4-10 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two equally good candidate methods obtain validation accuracies .81 and .85 due partly to sampling noise. Why may the chosen .85 overstate quality?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Selecting the winner favors favorable validation fluctuations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Even equal true performers can rank differently in a finite sample. Choosing the highest score introduces a winner&#x27;s-selection effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-11": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>11. Q4-11 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A multiplicative seasonal factor is 1.25 and observed response is 250. What is the deseasonalized observation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 200</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Divide observation by factor: 250/1.25=200. This removes the modeled proportional seasonal boost.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-12": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>12. Q4-12 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which uses of test data preserve its final evaluation role?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Compute a previously selected evaluation metric; B. Apply a locked preprocessing transformation; D. Apply the already selected model</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Scoring a fixed procedure is legitimate testing. Adapting the procedure after inspecting test outcomes breaks the independence needed for final evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-13": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>13. Q4-13 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With S=9, x=11, mu=10, C=0 and alarm rule S_new&gt;T for T=10, is there an alarm now?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. No; the new statistic equals the threshold but does not exceed it</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The new statistic is ten. Read the stated strict inequality carefully; implementations can alternatively specify &gt;=, but this question uses &gt;. The updated statistic is max(0,9+11-10-0)=10. The stated alarm rule is strictly greater than 10, so equality does not trigger it. Read the exact inequality rather than assuming every threshold is inclusive.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-14": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>14. Q4-14 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Three nearest neighbors have numeric responses 4, 10, and 13. What is the standard k-NN regression prediction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 9</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Average numeric responses: (4+10+13)/3=9. Regression does not require a class vote.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-15": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>15. Q4-15 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which residual plots suggest different modeling issues?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Long same-sign runs over time can suggest serial dependence; C. Systematic curvature can suggest wrong functional form; D. A funnel can suggest nonconstant error variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Diagnostics address conditional errors and structure. Marginal normality of one predictor does not prove linearity, independence, or equal error variance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Residual plots and regression assumptions:</strong> Separate nonlinearity, unequal residual spread, dependence, and unusually influential observations. A curved pattern suggests a mean-model problem; changing spread suggests a variance problem. Assumptions concern errors conditional on predictors rather than requiring every predictor to be normally distributed.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression diagnostics lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-16": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>16. Q4-16 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A straight-line fit misses a clear quadratic pattern in residuals. What is a reasonable model revision?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Add and validate a quadratic predictor column</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Systematic residual curvature suggests missing nonlinear structure. A polynomial column still permits linear-in-coefficients least squares.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Residual plots and regression assumptions:</strong> Separate nonlinearity, unequal residual spread, dependence, and unusually influential observations. A curved pattern suggests a mean-model problem; changing spread suggests a variance problem. Assumptions concern errors conditional on predictors rather than requiring every predictor to be normally distributed.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression diagnostics lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-17": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>17. Q4-17 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which behaviors are expected within a standard k-means run under its squared-distance objective?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The objective need not reach the global optimum; B. Updating a nonempty cluster to its mean cannot increase its assigned-point SSE; D. Assigning points to closest centers cannot increase WSS</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each alternating step improves or preserves the common objective. Nonincrease within a run does not establish global optimality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-18": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>18. Q4-18 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which claims about PCA information and interpretation are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Uncorrelated components need not be statistically independent; B. Loadings express each component as a combination of original features; C. Omitting a component can discard response-relevant information</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA optimizes a feature-space representation, not a supervised response objective. Independence is stronger than zero correlation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-19": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>19. Q4-19 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which tasks fit the lectures&#x27; descriptive examples?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assess whether a process reliability measure changed; B. Estimate associations between factors and past sales; C. Identify groups of similar customers</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first three characterize observed systems or patterns. Choosing an optimized shipment plan is prescriptive, though it can use descriptive and predictive results.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q4-20": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>20. Q4-20 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which goals can clustering support in the lectures?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Targeted marketing segments; C. Initial investigation of different subpopulations; D. Geographic facility placement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering discovers feature-based groupings. Supervised yes/no modeling uses known outcomes and is a different task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-21": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>21. Q4-21 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish smoothing from forecasting?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A current smoothed level can use the current observed value; B. Scoring a smoothed value as if it were an advance forecast can be optimistic; D. A forecast for the current value must be produced before observing it</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Information timing matters. Forecast evaluation must compare predictions made without the target observation against its later realization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-22": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>22. Q4-22 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model predicts well but uses information unavailable when the decision must be made. What is the most serious issue?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its apparent performance does not represent a deployable procedure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An evaluation must respect the information available at prediction time. Otherwise the reported predictive success is operationally misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q4-23": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>23. Q4-23 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions are supported by a CUSUM alarm?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Accumulated evidence has crossed the chosen threshold; B. A false alarm remains possible; C. The evidence is consistent with a shift relative to the baseline</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A detected statistical change does not prove a mechanism or eliminate the chance of random triggering.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-24": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>24. Q4-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For y=-1 and f(x)=.3, what is hinge loss?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 1.3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The signed margin y*f=-.3 is on the wrong side. Hinge loss is 1-(-.3)=1.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-25": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>25. Q4-25 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classification tree has leaves correctly classifying 18/20, 14/20, and 1/1 training observations. What is total training accuracy?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 33/41</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sum correct counts and total counts: (18+14+1)/(20+20+1)=33/41. Averaging leaf percentages equally would overweight the singleton.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-26": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>26. Q4-26 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A medicine-delivery dataset contains real rare delays caused by storms. Which approaches can preserve relevant risk?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assess how excluding delays changes operational optimism; B. Model disruption probability separately from ordinary delivery duration; C. Retain delays if the target includes such conditions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Rare adverse outcomes may matter greatly to decisions. Their treatment should reflect the target system and loss rather than aesthetic cleanliness.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-27": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>27. Q4-27 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are legitimate distinctions between ARIMA and GARCH?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. GARCH primarily models conditional variance; B. GARCH uses squared shocks in its variance recursion; C. ARIMA primarily models the conditional level/mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> GARCH&#x27;s variance structure has lagged variances and squared innovations, without an ARIMA-style differencing parameter in the lecture formulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — GARCH models variance, not the mean response:</strong> Separate the conditional mean from conditional variance. In a GARCH(1,1) update, both the previous squared shock and previous variance matter. Squaring removes shock direction. A variance forecast can stay large after a small shock when past variance is large.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q4-28": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>28. Q4-28 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A boxplot flags a value outside chosen whisker limits. What does this establish?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It is a candidate unusually extreme value under that convention</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Flagging is a diagnostic step, not a conclusion about validity or causation. Whisker conventions themselves differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-29": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>29. Q4-29 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A min-max scaler is trained on values from 0 to 10. Which statements about future x=15 are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It lies outside the original training range; B. Min-max fitting did not guarantee bounds for all future observations; D. Its transformed value is 1.5</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fitted linear map sends 15 to 1.5. Clipping is an additional modeling policy, not an automatic consequence of the scaling formula.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q4-30": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>30. Q4-30 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-fold CV follow from its construction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Average scores can compare candidate settings; B. Each fit excludes the held-out fold&#x27;s outcomes from training; D. Every fold is held out in turn</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CV is a resampling evaluation procedure. It does not mandate using the intermediate fits as an ensemble.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-31": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>31. Q4-31 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forest makes high-quality predictions but its variable-importance chart lacks effect direction. What is the best interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Importance summarizes contribution but is not a signed coefficient or causal mechanism</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Aggregate importance measures do not directly express slope sign, interactions, or intervention effects. They are useful summaries with limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-32": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>32. Q4-32 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about Box-Cox and regression assumptions are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Lambda is a transformation parameter; B. Normality relevant to conventional regression inference concerns errors conditional on predictors; D. The standard Box-Cox family requires positive input values</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Transformation choice and residual diagnostics are separate from raw marginal appearance. A normal-looking response histogram does not prove conditional-error assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-33": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>33. Q4-33 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Min-max scaling maps x to u in [0,1] on the training sample. To map the training minimum to -2 and maximum to 2, which formula is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. -2+4*u</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Use a+(b-a)*u with a=-2 and b=2. The endpoints become -2 and 2 as required.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q4-34": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>34. Q4-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ordinary trees and model trees are accurate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Leaf-specific regressions require enough observations to estimate coefficients; B. A model tree can fit a separate regression within a leaf; C. Ordinary regression CART often uses a constant leaf mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture contrasts conceptual leaf regressions with practical simple leaf predictions. They are distinct model variants. A constant-mean CART leaf estimates one response level. A leaf regression estimates multiple coefficients and therefore needs adequate observations and predictor variation. Descriptions of leaf-specific regressions do not imply every ordinary regression tree fits slopes.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-35": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>35. Q4-35 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For overlapping training classes, which statements about soft-margin SVM are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Some violations can be accepted; B. Class-specific loss multipliers can reflect asymmetric costs; C. Violations are balanced against margin regularization</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Soft-margin formulations allow loss rather than requiring perfect hard separation. Overlap need not make the optimization infeasible.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-36": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>36. Q4-36 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A 60/20/20 split of 1,500 records yields how many validation records?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 300</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Validation gets .2*1,500=300. These proportions are illustrative, not a universal requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-37": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>37. Q4-37 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> ARIMA(0,1,0) without drift has latest observation 42. What is its one-step conditional mean forecast?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 42</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A drift-free random walk predicts its next level as the current level because future innovations have mean zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q4-38": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>38. Q4-38 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which choices must be distinguished when specifying Holt-Winters?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Cycle length in observations; B. Additive versus multiplicative seasonal form; D. Observation frequency</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Frequency determines the units of L, and seasonal form determines whether effects are offsets or proportions. Response categories are not a seasonal cycle setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-39": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>39. Q4-39 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A call-center model predicts the number of arrivals in an interval from time of day and holiday status. Which introduced regression family naturally models this count mean?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Poisson regression</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Poisson regression relates a nonnegative count mean to predictors. Its distributional assumptions and exposure interval still need checking.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-40": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>40. Q4-40 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions are justified by ROC AUC=.9?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The scores have strong positive-versus-negative ranking under the evaluation distribution; C. It specifies neither a deployment threshold nor its cost</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AUC summarizes pairwise ranking over thresholds. It does not determine probability calibration, any single confusion matrix, or economic value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q4-41": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>41. Q4-41 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A two-dimensional weighted squared distance is 4*(dx1 squared)+1*(dx2 squared). For differences (1,2), what is the squared distance?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. 8</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Calculate 4*1+1*4=8. This weights the first dimension more strongly without confusing weights with observed feature values.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-42": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>42. Q4-42 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-NN model behavior are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its distance definition can change the selected neighbors; C. It stores/usefully retains labeled examples for prediction; D. Increasing k may smooth local variation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> k-NN classifies through local examples rather than fitting an SVM hyperplane. Its geometry and neighborhood size are substantive choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-43": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>43. Q4-43 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With n=100 and K=4, what is BIC if log-likelihood=-20? Use log(100)=4.605.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 58.42</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> BIC=-2*(-20)+4*4.605=40+18.42=58.42. The logarithm uses the specified natural-log value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-44": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>44. Q4-44 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The lecture gives roughly 40 observations as an ARIMA rule of thumb. How should this be interpreted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A heuristic, not a guarantee or universal minimum</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Data needs vary with order, dependence, noise, and intended forecasting. The lecture&#x27;s numerical guideline is practical intuition rather than a defining mathematical constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q4-45": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>45. Q4-45 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A cluster center is (5,5) and a very distant new point is assigned there because all other centers are even farther. What conclusion is justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. It is the nearest center, but practical cluster membership may still be questionable</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Nearest-center rules partition the entire feature space, including areas far from any observed cluster. Operational meaning requires more than relative proximity.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-46": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>46. Q4-46 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Permuting the names of clusters changes WSS when centers and memberships stay the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cluster identifiers are arbitrary. Relabeling the same partition does not change distances or fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-47": {"key": "4", "html": "<h2>47. Q4-47 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For upper CUSUM with previous statistic=0, observation=18, baseline=12, and allowance=2, the new statistic is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 4</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> max(0,0+18-12-2)=4.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-48": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>48. Q4-48 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A seasonal multiplicative factor below one necessarily reveals why that seasonal pattern occurs.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> It describes a proportional decrease in response at that phase. It does not identify the causal mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-49": {"key": "0.5; 50%", "html": "<h2>49. Q4-49 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If PCA eigenvalues are 5,3,2, the first component&#x27;s predictor-variance fraction is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.5; 50%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Divide its eigenvalue by the total: 5/(5+3+2)=.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-50": {"key": "0.8; 80%", "html": "<h2>50. Q4-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With TP=20,FN=5,FP=15,TN=60, specificity equals ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.8; 80%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Specificity conditions on actual negatives: 60/(60+15)=.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-01": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>1. Q5-01 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> PCA is fitted on training data. Which transformation should be used for a new test observation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Training means/scales and the learned loading matrix</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The learned coordinate system is part of the fitted pipeline. Recomputing it independently gives incompatible coordinates and may leak evaluation information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-02": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>2. Q5-02 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A verified weekly demand spike arose from a unique publicity event and the target is normal-week forecasting. What is the best justification for exclusion if the event is not expected to recur?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Its regime falls outside the stated modeling target, after investigation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture&#x27;s handling depends on the target and investigation. A real event can be excluded for a justified scope reason without pretending it was invalid.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-03": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>3. Q5-03 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which questions should precede outlier handling?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. What generated its unusual value?; C. Is the record valid?; D. Must the intended predictions or decisions cover that circumstance?</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Validity, origin, and relevance guide treatment. Optimizing a cosmetic fit metric alone can hide important system behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-04": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>4. Q5-04 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about AICc are correct for the formula AIC+2*K*(K+1)/(n-K-1)?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The stated denominator requires n&gt;K+1; C. For fixed K it approaches AIC as n grows; D. It adds a finite-sample complexity correction</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AICc increases the penalty when data are limited relative to estimated parameters. The formula is not valid with a zero or negative denominator.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-05": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>5. Q5-05 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Police patrols cannot cross a river except at distant bridges. Why might straight-line clustering distance be inadequate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Geometric proximity may not reflect actual travel accessibility</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The chosen metric should approximate operational similarity or travel. Geographic barriers can make direct distance misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-06": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>6. Q5-06 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about a cluster-based facility plan are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The number of facilities can constrain k; C. A center is an analytical candidate, not automatically a feasible building site; D. Coverage and travel constraints may require additional analysis</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering supports planning but may not directly encode budgets, allowed sites, travel networks, or coverage limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-07": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>7. Q5-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression-tree split yields child groups of 2 and 18 observations. Which comparison aligns with total squared-error fit?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Child SSE total, or variance contributions weighted by group size</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Groups contribute in proportion to their records. An unweighted sum of variances can misrepresent overall error when sizes differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q5-08": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>8. Q5-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Reliability is measured as operating hours before failure for each successive manufactured item; production batches contain 150 items. Which interpretations are supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A seasonal factor .7 at a phase represents a lower proportional response; B. Cycle length can be 150 observations; D. A positive trend means modeled reliability response rises over successive items</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Index units and response meaning determine interpretation. Trend and seasonal patterns describe associations, not mechanisms by themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-09": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>9. Q5-09 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two standardized predictors are perfectly identical. What is expected of the second principal component&#x27;s variance?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Zero</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> All feature variation lies along the common direction. The orthogonal contrast between identical features has zero variance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-10": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>10. Q5-10 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A specification is chosen with eight-fold CV on 1,600 non-test records. How many of those records fit the final model under the lecture&#x27;s refitting approach?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 1,600</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each CV fit used 1,400, but the final refit uses all 1,600 non-test observations. The separate test set remains reserved.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-11": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>11. Q5-11 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which features make a modeling conclusion useful and defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its limitations and assumptions are understood; B. It answers the actual operational question; C. It respects the information and population represented by the data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lectures emphasize matching models to real situations and interpreting output honestly. Agreement with prior beliefs is not a validation criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q5-12": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>12. Q5-12 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team fits all candidate models using only training data, but chooses predictors once using all labels before splitting. Why is its later validation suspect?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Outcome information from validation influenced feature selection</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Feature selection is part of training/selection. Splitting afterward does not undo leakage from previously examined outcomes.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-13": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>13. Q5-13 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A GARCH update has zero signed shock but a large previous variance. Can the new modeled variance still be large?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Yes, because lagged variance also contributes</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> GARCH carries variance persistence as well as squared-shock effects and a baseline term. One quiet observation need not erase high volatility.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — GARCH models variance, not the mean response:</strong> Separate the conditional mean from conditional variance. In a GARCH(1,1) update, both the previous squared shock and previous variance matter. Squaring removes shock direction. A variance forecast can stay large after a small shock when past variance is large.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q5-14": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>14. Q5-14 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The seasonal update has gamma=.25, x_t=180,current level=150,previous same-phase factor=1.0. What is its new multiplicative factor?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 1.05</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The observed relative factor is 180/150=1.2. Blend .25*1.2+.75*1.0=1.05.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-15": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>15. Q5-15 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A series&#x27; mean grows linearly while its first differences fluctuate around a stable mean. Which ARIMA component addresses this issue most directly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The differencing order d</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Differencing transforms levels into changes and can stabilize a trending mean. AR and MA terms then model the dependence of the transformed series.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q5-16": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>16. Q5-16 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In one dimension, points 0,2,9,11 are assigned to centers 1 and 10. What is total WSS?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. 4</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each point is one unit from its center, so four squared deviations sum to four.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-17": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>17. Q5-17 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In a calibrated binary model with false-positive cost 3 and false-negative cost 9, correct decisions free, what positive threshold minimizes stated expected error cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. .25</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Predict positive when (1-p)*3&lt;=p*9, giving p&gt;=3/(3+9)=.25. Costs determine the threshold under the stated assumptions. The expected positive-action loss is 3*(1-p); the negative-action loss is 9*p. They are equal at p=.25. Above that probability, classify positive; at equality, either action has the same expected stated cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-18": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>18. Q5-18 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two candidate neighbor rules have validation costs $12 and $8 per 100 decisions despite accuracies .95 and .90 respectively. If minimizing this measured cost is the objective, which is favored?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The .90-accuracy rule with $8 cost</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The chosen objective is decision cost. Different error types can make a lower-accuracy model more valuable.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-19": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>19. Q5-19 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Batches contain 150 items and take 6 to 8 days. Responses are indexed by item sequence across consecutive batches. Which variable is x_t in a reliability analysis?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Observed reliability response for item t</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> x_t is the observed outcome, such as operating hours before first failure. Sequence defines the index/cycle, not the response itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-20": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>20. Q5-20 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For observations 12,12,12 with baseline 10,C=1 and S0=0, what is the upper CUSUM after all three observations?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each observation adds 12-10-1=1, and none resets. The successive statistics are one,two,three.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-21": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>21. Q5-21 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about high-dimensional PCA are useful?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Loadings have one entry per original feature; B. Scores have one entry per observation for each component; C. At most min(n-1,p) components can have nonzero variance for centered data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Centering limits rank, loadings describe directions, and scores describe projections of records. None requires response labels.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-22": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>22. Q5-22 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which scenarios call for caution with multiplicative seasonal smoothing?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Observations include meaningful negative values; C. Level estimates approach zero in ratio updates; D. Seasonal variation is a roughly fixed additive amount</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ratios need appropriate denominators and interpretations. If seasonal amplitude is fixed in units, an additive form may be more suitable; positive well-behaved levels are not themselves a warning.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-23": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>23. Q5-23 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which random-forest claims are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Averages can reduce noise sensitivity when tree errors differ; C. A large forest is generally harder to explain than one small tree; D. Bootstrap and feature randomness diversify trees</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Averaging helps variance but cannot repair a flawed evaluation or training population. More trees reduce Monte Carlo fluctuation rather than eliminating all model limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests, bootstrap samples, and feature subsampling:</strong> Separate sampling observations with replacement from randomly limiting candidate features at each split. The second step reduces similarity among trees. Classification uses aggregated votes or probabilities; regression averages numerical predictions. Importance describes predictive usefulness, not causal direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-24": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>24. Q5-24 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements correctly contrast feature selection and feature extraction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Extraction can reduce dimension without selecting original columns directly; C. Selection retains a subset of original variables; D. PCA extraction constructs linear combinations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A component typically combines multiple features. Selection and extraction can both reduce dimensionality through different representations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA: scores, loadings, and outcome relevance:</strong> Draw a rotated coordinate system. Scores are projections; loadings describe directions. Components maximize predictor variance subject to orthogonality, without consulting y. Retaining all components preserves the centered feature information; truncating them may discard predictive signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-25": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>25. Q5-25 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinic has labeled records and wants estimated probability of infection from symptoms. Which lecture model is the most direct choice?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Logistic regression</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Logistic regression estimates a binary-outcome probability from features. Clustering, change detection, and PCA do not directly solve that stated task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Logistic probabilities versus odds:</strong> Convert eta to p=1/(1+exp(-eta)). Odds=p/(1-p); a one-unit predictor change multiplies odds by exp(beta) in an additive logistic model. It does not multiply probability by the same factor. A probability model and a classification threshold are separate choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-26": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>26. Q5-26 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which contrasts between time-series methods are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Simple smoothing extrapolates a level without explicit trend; B. Holt-Winters adds a repeating seasonal component; D. Holt adds an evolving trend</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The smoothing variants progressively add components. GARCH models conditional variance rather than grouping observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-27": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>27. Q5-27 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A separator has positive class for x1+x2&gt;6. A case moves from (2,3) to (2,5). What happens?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. It crosses from negative to positive</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The score sum changes from five to seven, crossing six. Both features enter this separator, unlike the vertical/horizontal special cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-28": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>28. Q5-28 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A coefficient estimate is -4 with an approximate 95% interval [-9,1]. Which inference is appropriate for a matching two-sided 5% test?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Zero is not excluded by the interval</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The interval includes zero, so it does not reject a zero coefficient at the matching level. Failure to reject is not proof that the effect is absent.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-29": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>29. Q5-29 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If class prevalence changes but sensitivity and specificity stay fixed, which metrics generally can change?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Overall accuracy; B. Precision; C. Expected cost per observation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Precision, accuracy, and cost mix performance with class frequencies. Sensitivity is conditioned on actual positives and is held fixed in the question. Sensitivity and specificity condition on actual class membership. Precision mixes class prevalence with those conditional rates: PPV=prevalence*sensitivity/[prevalence*sensitivity+(1-prevalence)*(1-specificity)]. Accuracy also mixes them: prevalence*sensitivity+(1-prevalence)*specificity.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-30": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>30. Q5-30 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An axle monitor tolerates some false alarms to detect failures before they happen. What tradeoff is it making?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Earlier warning versus unnecessary interventions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CUSUM sensitivity balances detection delay with false alarms. Costs of missed or late failures can justify intervention before certainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-31": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>31. Q5-31 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Using alpha=.3, previous level=50, previous trend=2, current observation=60, and no seasonality, what is the Holt level update?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 54.4</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Blend observation with the projected previous baseline: .3*60+.7*(50+2)=18+36.4=54.4.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-32": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>32. Q5-32 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are distinct choices in a k-NN classification specification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Distance metric; C. Number of neighbors; D. Feature scaling/weights</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first three define neighborhoods and their size. Innovation lags belong to time-series models, not ordinary k-NN.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-33": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>33. Q5-33 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For cancer defined as positive, TP=8,FN=2,FP=90,TN=900. What is accuracy, and what concern remains?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. 90.8%; precision is only about 8.2%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Accuracy=(8+900)/1,000=.908. Precision=8/(8+90)≈.0816, showing that high accuracy can coexist with many false positive predictions in a rare class.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-34": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>34. Q5-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> At prevalence=.2, sensitivity=.75,specificity=.9, false-negative cost=$20 and false-positive cost=$1, correct costs zero, what is expected cost per observation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. $1.08</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Miss cost=.2*.25*20=$1.00; false-alarm cost=.8*.1*1=$.08; total=$1.08.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision costs and the probability threshold:</strong> Compare expected losses for the two actions. With calibrated probability p, false-positive cost a and false-negative cost b, classify positive when a*(1-p)&lt;b*p, equivalently p&gt;a/(a+b). This relies on correct decisions having zero cost; more general costs change the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-35": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>35. Q5-35 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which can explain a correlation without a direct causal effect of x on y?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Reverse causation; C. Selection or omitted-variable structure; D. A common cause affects both</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Statistical association can arise through several pathways. A significance test alone cannot establish an intervention effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — P-values, effect sizes, confidence, and prediction:</strong> Interpret a p-value as a tail probability under the null model, not the probability the null is true. Distinguish statistical evidence from practical effect size and causality. A confidence interval for the mean response and a prediction interval for a new individual answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-36": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>36. Q5-36 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An operations team asks whether a new scanner reduced past queues, then asks how to staff tomorrow. Which pairing describes these questions?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Descriptive assessment followed by a prescriptive decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Assessing an observed change is descriptive; staffing chooses actions. A tomorrow-arrival forecast can sit between the two stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q5-37": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>37. Q5-37 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can a single holdout estimate vary materially across random splits?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Each fit may change when its training subset changes; B. Different splits allocate rare or influential cases differently; C. Finite validation sets contain random variation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Both fitted models and evaluation samples change across splits. Resampling can make selection more stable but cannot remove all uncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-38": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>38. Q5-38 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which choices distinguish the advanced introduced regression methods?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Spline knot placement determines where polynomial pieces meet; C. Poisson means may depend on exposure and explanatory factors; D. Bayesian priors encode distributions over unknown quantities</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> These methods use different representations and assumptions. Neighbor regression is local and does not require a global parametric response equation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Introduced regression families:</strong> Know the lecture-level distinctions: Poisson for count means, Bayesian updating of a parameter distribution, splines as joined polynomial pieces, and neighbor regression as local averaging. Use companion lectures to clarify a distinction rather than treating every advanced implementation detail as required scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression, nonlinear-model, and Bayesian companion lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-39": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>39. Q5-39 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A trained model is sensitive to a unit change from dollars to thousands of dollars. Which explanations can be relevant?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. An unadjusted distance metric changes its numerical geometry; B. An unadjusted norm penalty can change its relative feature treatment; D. Consistent preprocessing can address the scale change</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Distance- and penalty-based methods depend on representation. A consistent scale policy helps avoid arbitrary unit effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q5-40": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>40. Q5-40 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which choices could reasonably improve the interpretation of a CUSUM study?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Check sensitivity to plausible C and T values; C. Report a narrow conclusion matching the measured outcome; D. Use a baseline appropriate to the unchanged regime</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Baseline, settings, and scope affect the inference. Change detection identifies evidence relative to a model, not arbitrary broad causal claims.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-41": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>41. Q5-41 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A fitted regression uses log(y) as its response. What should be checked before interpreting a prediction on the raw y scale?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The inverse transformation and whether a conditional-mean bias adjustment is needed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Exponentiating a predicted log value does not automatically yield the conditional arithmetic mean of y. Response transformations change interpretation and require thoughtful reversal. Exponentiating a predicted conditional mean of log(y) generally does not recover E[y|X]. Under a lognormal error model, the latter includes an exp(sigma_squared/2) factor. Determine whether the prediction represents a median or a mean and whether a bias correction is appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Box-Cox, detrending, and returning to original units:</strong> For positive y, use (y^lambda-1)/lambda, with log(y) as the zero-power limit. A transformation can help a model without guaranteeing all assumptions. Re-add a removed trend after predicting detrended values. Nonlinear inverse transformations need care when interpreting conditional means.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-42": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>42. Q5-42 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which claims about a final test score are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It may fail to represent deployment after a substantial distribution shift; B. It still has finite-sample uncertainty; C. It estimates performance under the test sampling conditions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Independent testing assesses the selected method for a represented population, not all possible environments. Sample variability and changing conditions remain relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-43": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>43. Q5-43 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which interpretations of a large optimal WSS decrease are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Adding a cluster improves feature-space fit; B. The new group may merit substantive investigation; C. Operational relevance can differ from mathematical improvement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Unsupervised objective improvement is not proof of supervised labels or usefulness for a particular decision. Investigate what the group represents.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-44": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>44. Q5-44 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The same clinic now wants a positive/negative class prediction from labeled symptoms. Which lecture approaches can be used?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Classification trees; C. k-NN classification; D. SVM</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> SVM, neighbors, and trees directly use known response labels. Clustering discovers feature groups without those labels.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-45": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>45. Q5-45 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> One AIC value is five units above the minimum. What is its relative support under exp(-difference/2)?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Approximately .082</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Compute exp(-2.5), approximately .082. The lower-AIC model is the reference; this is relative support, not a posterior truth probability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — AIC, BIC, AICc, and likelihood:</strong> Use comparable responses, likelihood conventions, and parameter counts. Lower AIC/BIC is preferred. AIC=-2*logL+2*K; BIC=-2*logL+K*log(n). The AICc correction grows when n is small relative to K. A lower criterion does not certify causal interpretation or perfect prediction.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-46": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>46. Q5-46 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model family, a predictor specification, and a fitted numerical equation can all be called a model in these lectures.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture explicitly uses model at these three levels of detail.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q5-47": {"key": "1", "html": "<h2>47. Q5-47 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For w=(0,2) under the standard ±1 SVM margin normalization, the full margin width is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 1</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The norm is two, so full margin is 2/2=1.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM margins, hinge loss, and penalty conventions:</strong> Sketch f=0 and f=±1, label width 2/norm(w), and calculate hinge loss for margins above one, between zero and one, and below zero. Compare the placement of C and lambda in the stated objectives before predicting their effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-48": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>48. Q5-48 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A collective outlier must contain at least one individual value that is extreme without any context.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A sequence can be abnormal collectively while each individual value is plausible. Missing expected events are a lecture example.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-49": {"key": "autoregressive/value; innovation/error", "html": "<h2>49. Q5-49 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> ARIMA(p,d,q) uses p for ____ lags and q for ____ lags.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> autoregressive/value; innovation/error</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> p counts lagged transformed values and q lagged innovations, while d is the differencing order.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q5-50": {"key": "0.32", "html": "<h2>50. Q5-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For a node with class fractions .8 and .2, Gini impurity is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.32</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Compute .8*.2+.2*.8=.32, smaller than the balanced binary impurity .5.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, leaf predictions, impurity, and pruning:</strong> Follow split rules in sequence. Ordinary squared-error regression CART predicts a leaf mean; a model tree can fit a regression inside a leaf. Classification impurity and regression SSE are different criteria. More leaves can improve training fit while hurting held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}};
